    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda CRF1150L Africa Twin Enduro Sports Is a Tongue Twister, Weighs Less but Offers More\nFord Police Interceptor Utility Recalled Over Map Lights That Refuse To Turn Off\n7 photos\nPhoto: Ford\nClearly derived from the civilian-spec Explorer, the Police Interceptor Utility has been hit with a recall over a rather strange electrical issue. As it happens, a startup diagnostic may switch on both front map lights. Not a biggie per se, but the problem here is that said startup diagnostic may also leave the overhead console unable to turn those map lights off.\nFord is recalling an estimated 33,578 examples of the Police Interceptor Utility to fix this concern, a condition that violates federal lighting requirements. According to the Dearborn-based automaker, the power distribution box performs a diagnostic check at startup. The check briefly raises voltage on the interior lighting circuit, and the overhead console may not finish initializing all of its electronics when this happens.\nThe microcontroller, therefore, could mistake the electrical signal for a 'Global On' command. Dealer software and third-party tools are used by service technicians to trigger this command in order to verify that the body control module is properly sending power and signals to all interior lights, making it easier than pressing all interior light switches.\nFord explains in the recall documentation that the individual buttons for the front map lights become ineffective, leaving the driver and front passenger unable to switch off said lights. 26V726 is what the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration calls this campaign, with 26C50 being the automaker's number for it. Production dates for the affected vehicle population range from March 3, 2025, through October 23, 2026.\nThis also means that both 2026 and 2027 models are being recalled. Because the Police Interceptor Utility features unique overhead console hardware and software, this campaign doesn't include the Explorer. As for the regulatory issue, Ford points the finger at FMVSS 101.\nPhoto: Ford Pro Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standard 101 governs controls and displays, and section S5.3.4(a) requires a means to switch off any source of illumination in the front row. The phantom 'Global On' command makes those switches ineffective, creating a noncompliance condition. And yes, section S5.3.4(a) understandably requires all controls to perform their intended function.\nAs for the safety risks associated with driving with the map lights on, interior glare comes to mind. The Chicago Assembly Plant Vehicle Team identified this problem in June 2026, and the automaker's Critical Concern Review Group immediately opened an investigation. In the first instance, the safety boffins concluded that the issue would not develop while driving.\nCuriously enough, Ford also decided that the condition did not constitute regulatory noncompliance. That interpretation would be proved wrong in August 2026, when Ford started discussing the problem with the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration. Worse still, Ford became aware of 69 warranty claims in the US market by the end of October 13, 2026.\nThe fix depends on the console's production date. Dealers will replace overhead consoles in vehicles produced before July 9, 2026, while those from July 10 through October 23 will be inspected for an SW2 marking. Any console that carries the marking in question will be reinstalled at no charge to affected law enforcement, government agencies, and other customers.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nford police interceptor utility ford explorer Ford recall safety\nDownload: Ford Police Interceptor Utility map lights recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Aston Martin Brings the CoD: Modern Warfare 4 Dreadnought Into the Real World, To Race It in Dakar",
